---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const base = 'https://www.dg-sdk.com';
const url = base + '/blog/ionizing-bar-line-speed-travel-time';
const title = 'Ionizing Bar Line Speed: Separate Web Width from Travel Time';
const seoTitle = 'Ionizing Bar Line Speed: Web Width vs Travel Time';
const description = 'Calculate web travel time from distance and line speed, distinguish it from transverse bar span, and see why the result does not establish ionizer performance.';
---

<Layout title={seoTitle + ' | Shidike'} description={description} canonical={url}
  image="/assets/img/blog/web-travel-time-v1.jpg">
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: base },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Blog', item: base + '/blog' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: title, item: url }
    ]
  })} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BlogPosting', headline: title, description,
    image: base + '/assets/img/blog/web-travel-time-v1.jpg',
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Shidike', url: base },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Shidike', legalName: 'Dongguan Shidike Intelligent Equipment Co., Ltd.', url: base },
    datePublished: '2026-11-08', dateModified: '2026-11-08', mainEntityOfPage: url
  })} />

  <article class="post">
    <header class="post-head">
      <div class="container post-col">
        <div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/blog">Blog</a><span>/</span>Ionizing Bar Line Speed</div>
        <div class="post-tags"><span class="blog-tag">Static Control</span><span class="blog-tag">Ionizing Bars</span><span class="blog-tag">Line Speed</span></div>
        <h1>{title}</h1>
        <p class="post-lead">When reviewing an ionizing bar for moving film, separate two directions on the drawing: the span across the web and the distance along its travel. Only the second belongs in a travel-time calculation. A worked example shows what this calculation can tell you before an application trial.</p>
        <div class="post-info"><span>November 8, 2026</span><span>Shidike Technical Team</span><span>4 min read</span></div>
      </div>
    </header>
    <div class="container post-col post-body">
      <picture class="post-figure">
        <source media="(max-width: 600px)" srcset="/assets/img/blog/web-travel-time-v1-mobile.webp" type="image/webp" width="800" height="1400" />
        <source media="(max-width: 600px)" srcset="/assets/img/blog/web-travel-time-v1-mobile.jpg" type="image/jpeg" width="800" height="1400" />
        <source srcset="/assets/img/blog/web-travel-time-v1.webp" type="image/webp" />
        <img src="/assets/img/blog/web-travel-time-v1.jpg" alt="Conceptual top view of a moving web: distance L runs from A to B along travel, while bar span W runs across the web; travel time is L divided by constant speed v." width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      </picture>
      <p><em>Original geometric diagram. A and B are reference positions, not measured boundaries of an ionization zone. No installation dimensions or performance results are shown.</em></p>

      <section id="two-directions">
        <h2>Keep transverse span separate from travel distance</h2>
        <p>In this top-view example, the bar crosses the web. Its transverse span is labelled <em>W</em>. A point on the material moves from A to B along a different axis; that path length is <em>L</em>. Dividing <em>W</em> by line speed would time a distance the material point does not travel in this arrangement.</p>
        <p>For a layout review, mark A and B on the drawing and state why those positions were chosen. Treat them as geometric references unless application evidence establishes something more. A bar drawing alone does not define a sharply bounded treatment footprint on the material.</p>
        <p>Our <a href="/blog/ionizing-bar-retrofit-planning">ionizing-bar retrofit checklist</a> covers body dimensions, active span and mounting information. Here, the narrower question is how to calculate travel between two stated positions.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="travel-time-calculation">
        <h2>Calculate time with consistent units</h2>
        <p>For a material point moving at constant positive speed <em>v</em> over path length <em>L</em>:</p>
        <p class="formula">Travel time: t = L / v</p>
        <p>Use metres and metres per second to obtain seconds. If speed is reported in metres per minute, divide that speed by 60 first.</p>
        <p><strong>Invented arithmetic example:</strong> choose an A–B path length of 0.10 m. At 30 m/min, the speed is 0.50 m/s, so the point travels from A to B in 0.20 s. These values are calculation inputs, not recommended dimensions or machine settings.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Hypothetical constant-speed travel-time calculations">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Example</th><th scope="col">Path L</th><th scope="col">Line speed</th><th scope="col">Converted speed</th><th scope="col">Time L / v</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">First speed</th><td>0.10 m</td><td>30 m/min</td><td>0.50 m/s</td><td>0.20 s</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Twice the speed</th><td>0.10 m</td><td>60 m/min</td><td>1.00 m/s</td><td>0.10 s</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>The path stays unchanged, so doubling speed halves travel time. Increasing the bar's transverse span does not change either input in this calculation. For accelerating, stopping or reversing motion, a single nominal running speed does not describe the complete A–B transit; use the actual motion history or elapsed travel time.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="time-and-performance">
        <h2>Do not turn travel time into a line-speed rating</h2>
        <p>The calculation describes motion. It does not tell us the charge on the film at B, the ion balance of an ionizer, or how long adequate neutralization takes. Calling the A–B interval “dwell time” does not establish that useful ionization acts uniformly throughout it.</p>
        <p>The public scope of <a href="https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/68397" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IEC 61340-4-7:2025</a> separates ionizer measurements under specified conditions from qualification for a user's application. It identifies offset voltage and decay time as distinct measurements. This scope supports checking the actual application; it does not supply a maximum line speed for our example.</p>
        <p>An independent <a href="https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/indhealth/56/6/56_2018-0047/_article" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">charged-roll study by Wakatsuki and Sugimoto</a> investigated a passive ionizer with air assistance. The researchers measured charge-elimination current at different roll movement speeds and charge potentials. That apparatus-specific investigation illustrates why a moving-target question needs more evidence than geometry alone; it is not a performance report for a Shidike bar.</p>
        <p>Do not substitute a catalogue decay time for the calculated travel time and declare a passing speed. For reviewing the conditions behind those laboratory figures, see <a href="/blog/comparing-ionizer-datasheets">comparing ionizer datasheets</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="application-review">
        <h2>Use the calculation to clarify an equipment request</h2>
        <p>Attach a marked drawing and a short calculation line to the application brief: “A = ___; B = ___; path length = ___ m; constant speed = ___ m/s; calculated travel time = ___ s.” Keep the transverse span on a separate dimension line.</p>
        <p>Then ask the equipment and process teams what material measurements are needed at the intended operating speeds. Leave the application result open until that evidence exists. This makes the timing assumption reviewable without presenting a geometric estimate as demonstrated static-control performance.</p>
      </section>
      <div class="post-cta">
        <h2>Discuss your web path and intended speeds</h2>
        <p>Share the material, target face, marked path and operating-speed range when reviewing an ionizing-bar application with Shidike.</p>
        <p><a class="send-cta" href="/products/static-eliminator">Explore Static Eliminators &rarr;</a><a class="send-alt" href="/contact?type=selection&amp;product=Ionizing%20Bar%20Line%20Speed%20Review">Discuss your static-control application &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
  <style>
    .post-col { max-width: 780px; }
    .post-head { background: #f7f8fa; padding: 48px 0 34px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
    .post-head .breadcrumb { font-size: 13px; color: #7d8d97; margin-bottom: 18px; }
    .post-head .breadcrumb a { color: var(--brand-deep); text-decoration: none; }
    .post-head .breadcrumb span { margin: 0 8px; }
    .post-tags { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 6px; margin-bottom: 14px; }
    .blog-tag {
      font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .06em; text-transform: uppercase;
      background: #eef4f8; color: #4a6373; padding: 4px 9px; border-radius: 999px;
    }
    .post-head h1 {
      font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 3.4vw, 2.5rem); line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 800;
      letter-spacing: -.02em; color: var(--navy); margin: 0 0 14px;
    }
    .post-lead { font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.75; color: #46545f; margin: 0 0 18px; }
    .post-info { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 12.5px; color: #8a99a4; }

    .post-body { padding: 34px 0 70px; }
    .post-figure { display: block; margin: 0 0 30px; }
    .post-figure img {
      display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 8px;
    }
    .post-body h2 {
      font-size: 1.42rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -.01em; color: var(--navy);
      margin: 34px 0 12px;
    }
    .post-body p { font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.78; color: #3d4a54; margin: 0 0 15px; }
    .post-body a { color: var(--brand-deep); }
    .post-body h3 {
      font-size: 1.12rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); margin: 26px 0 10px;
    }
    .post-body ul, .post-body ol { margin: 0 0 15px; padding-left: 22px; }
    .post-body li { font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.78; color: #3d4a54; margin: 0 0 7px; }
    .post-body blockquote, .post-body .blog-quote {
      margin: 0 0 18px; padding: 13px 16px; background: #f2f6f9;
      border-left: 3px solid var(--brand-deep); border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
    }
    .post-body .blog-quote p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
    .post-body .step-block { margin: 0 0 22px; }
    .post-body .step-num {
      display: inline-block; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .06em;
      text-transform: uppercase; background: var(--navy); color: #fff;
      padding: 4px 10px; border-radius: 999px; margin-bottom: 8px;
    }
    .formula {
      font-size: 1.2rem !important; font-weight: 700; text-align: center; color: var(--navy) !important;
      background: #f2f6f9; border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px;
    }

    .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 0 0 20px; }
    .post-body table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14.5px; }
    .post-body th, .post-body td {
      text-align: left; padding: 10px 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); vertical-align: top;
    }
    .post-body thead th { background: #f2f6f9; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); }
    .post-body tbody th { width: 22%; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); background: #fafbfc; }

    .faq-list { display: flex; flex-direction: column; margin: 6px 0 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
    .faq-item { padding: 15px 4px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
    .faq-item summary { cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); font-size: 15.5px; }
    .faq-item p { margin: 10px 0 0; }

    .post-cta {
      margin-top: 40px; padding: 26px 26px 22px; background: #f7f8fa;
      border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 10px;
    }
    .post-cta h2 { margin-top: 0; font-size: 1.25rem; }
    .send-cta, .send-alt { display: inline-block; font-weight: 700; margin-right: 22px; }
    @media (max-width: 767px) {
      .post-body { padding-left: 16px; padding-right: 16px; }
    }
  </style>
</Layout>
